## Gadgetbridge reference

The BCS helper speaks a protocol whose layout was learned from the Gadgetbridge project
(https://codeberg.org/Freeyourgadget/Gadgetbridge), which is also AGPL-3.0-or-later, © the
Gadgetbridge contributors.

Two commits matter, and both are named so a reader can tell them apart:

- `de7c2ba8d636a3dc5b750770fb3654671a4bd260` — the Codeberg commit `docs/BCS_PHASE0.md` cites as the
  project's reference.
- `a0948ee1cbc2a870f91d313f8e37df5f524465f7` — the GitHub mirror's `master`, frozen at 2024-12-22,
  which is what the Phase 2 port was actually read from and line-cited against. Codeberg answered an
  automated fetch with a bot check and the Codeberg hash does not resolve on the mirror (HTTP 422),
  so the immutable mirror hash was used instead. `docs/BCS_NOTICE.md` records the same pair.

Phase 1 contains **no ported Gadgetbridge code**: every file there is original, written from the
design in `docs/BCS_PHASE0.md`. The only reference-source fact Phase 1 uses is the Huawei SDP
service UUID `82FF3820-8411-400C-B85A-55BDB32CF060`, cited in `docs/BCS_PHASE0.md` §1.1 as
`HuaweiConstants.java:33`, and used as a constant in `sidecar/bcs_bt.py` and
`src/huawei/constants.ts`. That UUID is now what a live run resolves the RFCOMM channel with:
`probe-channel` and the sidecar's `resolve_channel` ask BlueZ's `sdptool` which record carries it, and
the sidecar refuses to connect to any channel SDP did not name in that session. The SDP resolving,
matching and gating code is original; the only upstream fact behind it is the UUID itself.

Phase 2 (the packet codec under `src/huawei/`) **is** a port, and it holds no key material: it
frames bytes, parses TLVs and computes the checksum. Each ported file carries Gadgetbridge's own
AGPL header reference plus a line naming the upstream file, the line range and the commit it was read
from.

Phase 3 (trust/auth, also under `src/huawei/`) **is** a port too, and it is where the key material
enters: the crypto primitives, the auth packets and the HiChain state machine. It reads no file, holds
the key only in memory, zeroes it on completion or `destroy()`, and never passes key material to a
logger or to an error message — `test/authKeyLeak.test.ts` is the check for that last part. What it
does **not** port, deliberately: the bonding path (`HuaweiCrypto.createSecretKey(macAddress)`,
`DeviceConfig.Bond` / `BondParams`) and the legacy digest auth (HiChainLite, service `0x01` command
`0x13`). `docs/BCS_PHASE3.md` lists every divergence and every finding.

