`src/huawei/errors.ts` and `src/huawei/index.ts` are original. The error taxonomy is this project's,
because a live stream must produce typed errors rather than exceptions. The full list of deliberate
divergences is at the top of the file that makes them, and summarised in
`docs/BCS_NOTICE.md`.

One finding worth recording, because it was verified rather than assumed: `CheckSums.getCRC16` is
CRC-16/XMODEM — polynomial 0x1021, init 0x0000, MSB-first, no reflection, no final XOR. Evidence: the
ported loop reproduces the CRC of all five Gadgetbridge test frames (0x40B6, 0xA4F0, 0xCC98, 0xFAD3,
0x37CA), returns the published XMODEM check value 0x31C3 for `"123456789"`, and matches an
independent bit-by-bit implementation over the whole byte range and over seeded random input
(`test/huaweiCrc16.test.ts`). `docs/BCS_PHASE0.md` §1.3 notes the reflected polynomial 0xA001 of the
unrelated `getCRC16ansi` variant; the frame does **not** use it.

## Per-file header convention

Every source file in this helper opens with the same four items:

1. a one-line purpose statement that also states the write restriction (only allowlisted
  authentication frames may be sent to the watch),
2. `Copyright (C) 2026 HyperionOS Project. SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later`,
3. a pointer to this `NOTICE` file,
4. where applicable, the Gadgetbridge reference line above.

## Privacy rules carried into the code

- The watch's Bluetooth address is not treated as a secret, but it is never committed, never
  logged to a file in the repository, and never printed unmasked: every script and test masks
  it as `XX:XX:XX:XX:AA:BB`, keeping only the last two octets.
- No device auth code, cookie or account token is read, requested or stored in Phase 1.
- Bytes received from the watch are never logged; only the byte count and the time are.
- In Phase 3 the auth key is supplied by the caller as bytes. It is copied on construction, never
  logged, never put in an error message or a `detail`, and never sent to the watch; `destroy()` and a
  completed flow zero it, along with every intermediate secret. `test/authKeyLeak.test.ts` searches
  everything the machine produces — errors, rendered error objects and the bytes it asks the caller to
  send — for the key and for the derived key, in five spellings each.
- Part 3B's live script (`tools/bcs_auth.sh`, written and not run) reads the key from
  `~/.config/aether-bcs/auth-key.txt` with mode `0600` checked first, takes the pin code from a hidden
  prompt on stdin, never prints the derived key, and pipes every line it produces through a masker. It
  can also generate the key (`--set-key --generate`, 16 random bytes from `node:crypto`) and write it
  straight to that file: the value is never echoed, never in argv and never in a log.
- The Huawei account ID is personal data, kept in its own file (`account.txt`, mode `0600`) and typed
  at a hidden prompt by `--set-account`. It is never printed, logged or reported — only its digit count
  is — and `--with-account` refuses before the radio opens, because the account frame has to be
  encrypted with the derived key and that cipher is not implemented.
- One machine boundary is named rather than hidden: resolving the RFCOMM channel runs
  `sdptool browse <address>`, so the address is that one child process's argv for the length of the
  query. It is the only place the address leaves this helper unmasked, it is required by the tool, and
  nothing is written to disk by it. Every log line, event, report and error this helper produces is
  masked, and `mask_text()` in `sidecar/bcs_bt.py` is applied to every protocol line the sidecar emits.
- The same script can look the channel up without a run: `--sdp-only` requires `SDPLOOKUP` at the
  terminal (never `AUTHENTICATE`), reads **no** key, writes no identity file, opens no RFCOMM
  connection and sends no frame. It prints only the masked watch address and the SDP evidence, and
  leaves the same pair of private per-run files as a live run.
- Each run writes its own log and report under that state directory, and no run overwrites the one
  before it: the name carries a UTC stamp, plus the shell's pid when `tools/bcs_auth.sh` is the caller,
  and a driver started on its own adds its own pid when its default name is taken. The shell runs under
  `umask 077` and the driver creates the report with mode `0600`, so both files are private. A report
  holds stage names, coded refusals and byte/frame counts only — no key, no account value, no payload
  and no unmasked address.